    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `on_build_finished`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `on_build_finished` function is a callback that executes after a Sphinx documentation build process is completed. Its purpose is to display the top five slowest reading durations for documents generated during the build. The function takes two parameters: `app`, which is an instance of the Sphinx application, and `error`, which is an exception object if an error occurred during the build; otherwise, it is `None`. The function returns `None`.\n\nThe function retrieves the `duration` domain from the application's environment, which contains a dictionary of document names and their corresponding reading durations. If this dictionary is empty, the function returns early without doing anything. Otherwise, it sorts the dictionary items by duration in descending order and logs the top five document names along with their durations, formatted to three decimal places.\n\nThe function relies on the `sorted` function with `itemgetter` to sort the durations and `islice` to limit the output to the top five entries. It uses the `logger.info` method to print messages to the console. There are no explicit error handling mechanisms for exceptions; instead, the function assumes that any errors would have been caught earlier in the build process. The side effect of this function is that it outputs information to the console, which can be useful for performance analysis during the documentation build.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
